1
00:00:01,030 --> 00:00:06,556
Fifteen minutes. That's how long it took
a quiet river in Zion to carry nearly 50

2
00:00:06,596 --> 00:00:10,220
times more water, with seven
people inside the canyon.

3
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,275
September 14, 2015.

4
00:00:15,315 --> 00:00:17,995
A morning in Zion National Park.

5
00:00:18,035 --> 00:00:22,328
A group of seven canyoneers from
California and Nevada picks up

6
00:00:22,368 --> 00:00:26,220
a permit to descend a narrow
slot called Keyhole Canyon.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,276
At 2:22 that afternoon,
the National Weather

8
00:00:31,316 --> 00:00:34,065
Service issues a flash flood warning.

9
00:00:34,105 --> 00:00:36,200
The park closes its canyons.

10
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:42,640
Sometime between 3:30 and 4:30 that
afternoon, the group enters the slot.

11
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:48,300
Barely more than half an inch
of rain falls in about an hour.

12
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:52,330
And the North Fork of
the Virgin River goes

13
00:00:52,370 --> 00:00:55,985
from 55 cubic feet per second to 2,630.

14
00:00:56,025 --> 00:00:57,610
In fifteen minutes.

15
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:02,840
None of the seven came out alive.

16
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:08,640
More than 60 searchers spent over
1,000 hours looking for them.

17
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:11,699
High water and more rain
kept searchers out of

18
00:01:11,739 --> 00:01:14,425
the canyon's technical sections for days.

19
00:01:14,465 --> 00:01:17,161
The last of the seven was found that

20
00:01:17,201 --> 00:01:20,470
Thursday, downstream,
in Pine Creek Canyon.

21
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:24,930
And here's the part
that surprised us most.

22
00:01:24,970 --> 00:01:28,000
The Park Service says a
flow that high happens

23
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,910
on this river about
once every three years.

24
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:37,036
That same afternoon, south of the
park, in the town of Hildale, a wall

25
00:01:37,076 --> 00:01:41,175
of water came out of another canyon
and swept away two vehicles.

26
00:01:41,215 --> 00:01:43,380
Thirteen women and children died.

27
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:48,520
Twenty people, in a few
hours, in one corner of Utah.

28
00:01:52,290 --> 00:01:56,580
This is the other side of one of
America's most-visited canyons.

29
00:01:56,620 --> 00:02:01,475
The beauty that draws nearly five
million people a year is made by the

30
00:02:01,515 --> 00:02:05,920
same water, and the same rock,
that can turn deadly in minutes.

31
00:02:16,030 --> 00:02:20,780
To understand why Zion floods the way
it does, you have to look at the walls.

32
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:26,053
Zion's cliffs are Navajo
Sandstone, petrified

33
00:02:26,093 --> 00:02:29,155
sand dunes more than 2,000 feet thick.

34
00:02:29,195 --> 00:02:33,505
Bare rock doesn't soak up a
sudden storm. It sheds it.

35
00:02:33,545 --> 00:02:36,497
Every side canyon becomes
a funnel, and the

36
00:02:36,537 --> 00:02:39,320
water races toward the narrowest places.

37
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:45,805
The Virgin River drops 71 feet for
every mile it runs through the park.

38
00:02:45,845 --> 00:02:48,520
The Mississippi drops about an inch.

39
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:52,065
And this small river carries
about a million tons of

40
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:56,060
sediment out of Zion every
year, most of it during floods.

41
00:02:57,650 --> 00:03:00,983
Side canyons like this
one, Mystery Canyon, drop

42
00:03:01,023 --> 00:03:03,935
straight into the Narrows over waterfalls.

43
00:03:03,975 --> 00:03:06,580
Canyoneers rappel down them with ropes.

44
00:03:08,170 --> 00:03:11,480
In other words, the floods
aren't an accident in Zion.

45
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:13,440
They are how the canyon was made.

46
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:22,046
The same flash floods that carved these
walls over millions of years are still at

47
00:03:22,086 --> 00:03:27,300
work every summer, when monsoon storms
build over the plateau in the afternoon.

48
00:03:33,170 --> 00:03:36,300
The most famous hike in the
park is also the wettest.

49
00:03:36,340 --> 00:03:41,942
The geologist Grove Karl Gilbert, who
came through here in 1872 with the

50
00:03:41,982 --> 00:03:47,035
Wheeler Survey, is credited with
giving it its name: the Narrows.

51
00:03:47,075 --> 00:03:49,180
In the Narrows, the trail is the river.

52
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:53,449
The canyon walls rise 1,000
feet and more, and in places

53
00:03:53,489 --> 00:03:57,300
the river between them is
only 20 to 30 feet wide.

54
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:02,340
We hiked it from the top,
down, in November 2021.

55
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:07,440
Cold water, slippery rocks, and walls
so high you lose sight of the sky.

56
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:13,820
In a slot this narrow, when the water
rises, there is often nowhere to climb.

57
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:20,471
So the park closes the Narrows when
the river runs above 150 cubic feet

58
00:04:20,511 --> 00:04:24,610
per second, and whenever a flash
flood warning is in effect.

59
00:04:26,490 --> 00:04:29,800
The danger is that the storm
doesn't have to be where you are.

60
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:35,400
Rain can fall miles away, out of sight,
and the flood arrives under a blue sky.

61
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:42,200
In July 1998, a flash flood in
the Narrows killed two hikers.

62
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:46,351
And in August 2022, a
29-year-old hiker from

63
00:04:46,391 --> 00:04:50,125
Tucson, Jetal Agnihotri, was swept away.

64
00:04:50,165 --> 00:04:55,290
Her body was found three days later,
about six river miles downstream.

65
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:07,461
Some of Zion's canyons are so tight
that the only way through is down:

66
00:05:07,501 --> 00:05:11,290
rappelling over waterfalls,
swimming the pools between them.

67
00:05:13,110 --> 00:05:18,282
In July 1993, a church group from
Salt Lake City, three leaders

68
00:05:18,322 --> 00:05:22,750
and five teenage boys, set
out to descend Kolob Creek.

69
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:25,470
The water was much higher
than they expected.

70
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:30,433
One leader, Kim Ellis,
drowned in a whirlpool while

71
00:05:30,473 --> 00:05:33,540
trying to rescue another
leader, David Fleischer.

72
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:38,310
Later, Fleischer was swept over
another waterfall. He drowned too.

73
00:05:40,090 --> 00:05:44,325
The last adult and the five boys
climbed onto a ledge above the torrent.

74
00:05:44,365 --> 00:05:46,900
And they waited there. Four nights.

75
00:05:49,050 --> 00:05:52,465
Rescuers finally reached
them and winched them out of

76
00:05:52,505 --> 00:05:55,725
the canyon, almost 1,500
feet, then flew them out.

77
00:05:55,765 --> 00:05:57,250
All six survived.

78
00:05:59,090 --> 00:06:03,900
Families later sued the Park Service and
the county water district, arguing the

79
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:08,995
group should have been warned about water
being released from a reservoir upstream.

80
00:06:09,035 --> 00:06:13,690
In Zion, the water you see isn't
always the water that's coming.

81
00:06:19,530 --> 00:06:22,000
And sometimes the danger is invisible.

82
00:06:23,710 --> 00:06:27,415
On July 4, 2020, a dog
died about an hour after

83
00:06:27,455 --> 00:06:31,080
playing in the North Fork
of the Virgin River.

84
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:33,810
It had been eating floating mats of algae.

85
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:41,043
Park scientists found a cyanobacterium,
a microbe, producing a potent nerve

86
00:06:41,083 --> 00:06:46,090
toxin called anatoxin-a, at levels
far above what's considered safe.

87
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:52,047
Today the park monitors its
streams for toxic cyanobacteria,

88
00:06:52,087 --> 00:06:55,675
from the Narrows and the
Emerald Pools to the Subway.

89
00:06:55,715 --> 00:06:59,476
Don't drink the water untreated,
don't touch or swallow

90
00:06:59,516 --> 00:07:03,110
the algae, and never let a
dog drink from the river.

91
00:07:06,380 --> 00:07:11,385
The strange twist is that the same family
of microbes is everywhere in this desert.

92
00:07:11,425 --> 00:07:16,263
Cyanobacteria help form the dark
crust on Zion's soil, the living skin

93
00:07:16,303 --> 00:07:20,445
that holds the sand together
and keeps it from blowing away.

94
00:07:20,485 --> 00:07:23,320
In the soil, they build the desert.

95
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:27,310
In the river, in the wrong
conditions, they turn toxic.

96
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:35,800
Not all of Zion's danger comes from water.

97
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:37,340
Some of it comes from height.

98
00:07:39,130 --> 00:07:42,363
In 1916, a visiting
minister named a narrow

99
00:07:42,403 --> 00:07:45,635
fin of rock standing
high above the canyon.

100
00:07:45,675 --> 00:07:49,265
Only an angel, the story
goes, could land on it.

101
00:07:49,305 --> 00:07:51,940
The name stuck: Angels Landing.

102
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:56,970
Ten years later, the park
built a trail to the top.

103
00:07:57,010 --> 00:08:02,107
Under the park's acting superintendent,
crews cut it into the cliff with hand

104
00:08:02,147 --> 00:08:07,350
tools, including the tight stack of
switchbacks still called Walter's Wiggles.

105
00:08:09,030 --> 00:08:13,805
The last half mile follows a spine of
rock with sheer drops on both sides.

106
00:08:13,845 --> 00:08:16,709
For much of that stretch,
the only thing to

107
00:08:16,749 --> 00:08:19,720
hold on to is a chain
bolted into the stone.

108
00:08:21,530 --> 00:08:23,640
We climbed it in 2021.

109
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:27,410
The top stands nearly 1,500
feet above the river.

110
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:35,077
Researchers count at least 17
deaths at Angels Landing over

111
00:08:35,117 --> 00:08:38,855
the decades, more than a
dozen of them since 2000.

112
00:08:38,895 --> 00:08:43,616
One researcher who studies deaths in
national parks said that if it isn't

113
00:08:43,656 --> 00:08:47,700
the most dangerous trail in
America, it's one of the top five.

114
00:08:49,630 --> 00:08:52,805
Many of the falls didn't
even happen on the chains.

115
00:08:52,845 --> 00:08:57,725
They happened before or after them,
on stretches where people felt safe.

116
00:08:57,765 --> 00:09:03,360
The most recent death, in April 2026,
was a fall from the chained section.

117
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:08,562
By the late 2010s, so many
people were lining up on

118
00:09:08,602 --> 00:09:11,515
the chains that the
park changed the rules.

119
00:09:11,555 --> 00:09:15,244
Since April 2022, you need
a permit to climb Angels

120
00:09:15,284 --> 00:09:18,940
Landing, and the permits
are given out by lottery.

121
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:22,931
The park says the goal was to reduce

122
00:09:22,971 --> 00:09:25,745
crowding and make the
climb fairer and safer.

123
00:09:25,785 --> 00:09:31,065
In the first two years, it
issued nearly 400,000 permits.

124
00:09:31,105 --> 00:09:35,640
On the busiest days, the odds of
winning were as low as 8 percent.

125
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:41,310
The crowds left other traces, too.

126
00:09:41,350 --> 00:09:46,226
In 2021, park crews hauled almost
five tons of human waste off the

127
00:09:46,266 --> 00:09:50,660
toilets at Scout Lookout, the
saddle just below the chains.

128
00:09:56,290 --> 00:10:00,635
In 1920, fewer than 4,000
people visited Zion.

129
00:10:00,675 --> 00:10:04,820
In 2021, more than five
million did, a record.

130
00:10:06,580 --> 00:10:09,585
Most of them leave with
nothing but photographs.

131
00:10:09,625 --> 00:10:15,123
But the rangers here answer hundreds of
emergency calls every year, because the

132
00:10:15,163 --> 00:10:20,420
things that make Zion beautiful, the
water and the rock, never stop moving.

133
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:26,605
And sometimes, they move all at once.

134
00:10:26,645 --> 00:10:31,367
The flat valley floor where the park's
lodge stands today is the bottom of a

135
00:10:31,407 --> 00:10:35,480
lake, left behind when a mountainside
collapsed into this canyon.

136
00:10:37,230 --> 00:10:39,130
That's the next chapter. See you there.

